scope: |
  Finish `V2Fleet`'s value conversion. The plugin path landed in f30d11f05 and works — `mkdirs`
  answers on both lanes — but the fleet is OPT-IN because enabling it takes DIFF from 3 to 7, and
  one of those four is diagnosed as this: the adapter converts scalars, bytes and constructors and
  nothing else.

  MEASURED, ONE PROBE AT A TIME, WHICH IS HOW THE GAP WAS FOUND: `std-process-import` failed with
  `a host function was passed VData` (fixed in f30d11f05, both directions — v3 numbers a constructor
  and v2 names it), and now fails with `a host function was passed VMap`. The refusal is my own and
  it is doing its job: it names the shape rather than passing something wrong through.

  THREE SHAPES REMAIN AND ALL THREE ARE MECHANICAL, checked against v2 before claiming:
      VMap  <-> MapV      `MapV.from(entries)` / `.entries` (a VectorMap, insertion-ordered)
      VSet  <-> SetV      `SetV.from(elems)`   / `.elems`   (a LinkedHashSet)
      VArr  <-> ForeignV  v2 has no array case: `arr.new` answers
                          `ForeignV(ArrayBuffer[Value])`, so the handle is what crosses

  A CLOSURE STILL CANNOT CROSS, and that is not this claim's to fix: v3's `VClos` is a function
  index with captures and v2's `ClosV` is an env and code. A plugin taking a callback needs a
  different mechanism than value conversion, and the refusal must keep naming it.

  MEASUREMENT: with the fleet ENABLED, DIFF must fall — `std-process-import` is the case this
  unblocks. The floor is still 3 and the fleet still does not go on by default until it is met; the
  other three DIFFs are NOT diagnosed and are not claimed here.
  DONE: the three shapes convert, and a FIFTH gap the claim did not predict — EXCEPTIONS. A host
  function's failure is part of its contract: `std/fs.ssc`'s `listDir` on a missing directory is
  SUPPOSED to raise and the program catches it. v2 raises `SscThrow` and its own `try` catches it;
  here the plugin's Java exception escaped the executor and surfaced as
  `cannot read '<the .ssc>': NoSuchFileException` — a message about the SOURCE FILE, from a handler
  that assumes anything thrown came from reading it. Translated now: `SscThrow` crosses by value,
  anything else becomes a catchable throw carrying its message. All FOUR previously-DIFF cases then
  MATCH — individually, concurrently, and on both lanes.

  WIDENED 2026-08-15 to `v3/corpus-report.sh` and `v3/ssc3`, BEFORE the edit, because the report
  still counts those four DIFF and the cause is neither the adapter nor the cases.

  THE REPORT HAS ITS OWN COPY OF THE v2 INVOCATION and the two copies have now diverged. It builds
  the IR with the FRONT (whose classpath I extended, so the fleet IS installed and lowering emits
  `(prim "mkdirs" …)` instead of a refusal) and then runs that IR with
  `java -cp "$V2_CP" ssc.cli` — the plain entry, WITHOUT the fleet. So the build side and the run
  side disagree about which prims exist, and the program dies on a prim the builder was entitled to
  emit. `__v2-cp` returns `v2/src` classes and the toolchain and nothing else.

  That is a second decision site, the shape this repository keeps paying for: `v3/ssc3` learned
  about the fleet and `v3/corpus-report.sh` did not. The fix is for the report to use the same v2
  entry the driver does rather than to keep a parallel copy of the decision.
next: make the report use the driver's v2 entry, then re-measure with the fleet on
